---
import { linkInstagramMentions } from "../../utils/instagramMentions";
import ArtworkMeta from "../../components/art/ArtworkMeta.astro";

const metaFor = linkInstagramMentions("@stack_xyz");
const credits1 = linkInstagramMentions("<strong>Project Consultant</strong><br />@pasuthh");
const credits1b = linkInstagramMentions("<strong>Structure design</strong><br />@putpat13o");
const credits2 = linkInstagramMentions("<strong>Big thanks for folks that let me borrow their PCs</strong><br />@pasuthh<br />@msyves<br />@roma_or_am_i");
const credits3 = linkInstagramMentions("<strong>Special Thank</strong><br />@toeeyt<br />@tentaclesgallery<br />@alcoholidaysss<br />@uuunbbb<br />@poc9.s<br />@sthienwiwat");
const credits4 = linkInstagramMentions("<strong>Photos by</strong><br />@frameofchaos");
const photo = (n) => `/images/do-npcs-hear/npcs-${n}.jpg`;
const photoAlt = (n) => `Do The NPCs Hear The Simulated Wind — performance documentation ${n}`;
const credits5 = linkInstagramMentions("Organised by @stack_xyz and @team_stimulant , in collaboration with @goetheinstitut.thailand , with support from @thaifilmarchive , Office of the Prime Minister, Creative Economy Agency (CEA), Virtual Media Lab, immertech, and REAL Bangkok.");
---

<div class="mt-4 max-w-3xl">
  <ArtworkMeta fields={[
    { label: "Year", value: "2026" },
    { label: "Country", value: "Thailand" },
    { label: "Duration", value: "20 mins" },
    { label: "Setup", value: "4PCs, Aluminum profiles structure" },
    { label: "Date", value: "4 July 2026" },
    { label: "Venue", value: "Virtual Media Lab, 4th Floor, TCDC Bangkok (The Grand Postal Building)" },
    { label: "For", value: metaFor, html: true },
    { label: "Exhibition", value: "PATCH NOTES THAT REFUSE A SETTLED WORLD" },
    { label: "Curatorial Role", value: "Co-curator, as Stack" },
  ]} />

  <p class="mb-4">
    What happens when games become sites of archaeological listening? As preservation initiatives like
    <a href="https://archive.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Internet Archive</a><sup>1</sup>
    work to save digital culture, this investigation addresses the acoustic worlds embedded in games—procedurally
    generated soundscapes, layered atmospheres, hidden environmental recordings—that risk being lost over time,
    harder to archive as new techniques like ray-tracing reshape how virtual worlds are rendered.
  </p>

  <aside class="art-margin-note" id="field-recording-note">
    <p style="font-family: var(--f-data); font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;">
      <strong>Field recording</strong>: recording sound outside the studio, at the place where it happens.
      It began as a tool for ethnographers and naturalists, and became a way of composing and of documenting
      environments before they change.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <p class="mb-4">
    If we <a href="#field-recording-note" class="art-callout">field record</a> to capture the soundscapes of the real world, shouldn't we do the same for digital simulation sites?
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    This practice treats game worlds as sonic landscapes worth excavating. Not playing. Listening. Revealing the world-building beneath the surface.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    Do The NPCs Hear the Simulated Wind: four stations, raw sound to composed music.
  </p>

  <h2 class="kicker art-kicker mt-8 mb-4">
    Conceptual Framework and Context
  </h2>

  <p class="mb-4">
    Contemporary immersive games have quietly become sophisticated acoustic environments. Beneath their
    surfaces, procedural systems compose the world in real time, weather that shifts pressure and wind,
    ecosystems that breathe and call, populations that murmur and move. Much of this sound originates in
    actual field recordings: captured material from the physical world, re-embedded into the virtual.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    The systems that generate these sounds: weather, ecology, population, dialogue, material physics
    representing a form of invisible compositional architecture. They are designed to be felt but not examined.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    This practice inverts the intended relationship with play. Rather than inhabiting these worlds as a
    participant, we enter them as listeners, moving through engineered environments with the attention of a
    field recordist, extracting, cataloguing, and re-contextualising the sounds that constitute a game's
    hidden atmosphere.
  </p>

  <h2 class="kicker art-kicker mt-8 mb-4">
    Virtual Field Recording
  </h2>

  <aside class="art-margin-note" id="schafer-note">
    <p style="font-family: var(--f-data); font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;">
      <strong>R. Murray Schafer</strong> (1933–2021): Canadian composer and writer. He used
      &ldquo;soundscape&rdquo; for the whole acoustic environment as it is heard, and read it through
      keynote sounds (the constant background), signals (foreground sounds we attend to) and soundmarks
      (sounds unique to a place and its community).
    </p>
  </aside>

  <p class="mb-4">
    extends the tradition of acoustic ecology<sup>2</sup>
    (<a href="#schafer-note" class="art-callout">Schafer's soundscape theory</a><sup>3</sup>) into virtual space. Where field
    recordists once captured vanishing soundscapes of the physical world, this practice turns to immersive
    game environments. The practice inverts the act of play into an act of listening. Game environments
    become field sites treating virtual worlds as archaeological sites.
  </p>

  <div class="art-footnotes">
    <p><sup>1</sup>Internet Archive: a non-profit digital library founded by Brewster Kahle in 1996, based in San Francisco. Alongside the Wayback Machine it keeps playable software collections such as the <a href="https://archive.org/details/internetarcade" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Internet Arcade</a> (2014). See <a href="https://blog.archive.org/2024/11/21/vanishing-culture-preserving-gaming-history/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">&ldquo;Vanishing Culture: Preserving Gaming History&rdquo;</a>, Internet Archive Blogs (21 November 2024).</p>
    <p><sup>2</sup>Acoustic ecology studies the relationship between living beings and their environment through sound. It grew out of the <a href="https://www.sfu.ca/~truax/wsp.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">World Soundscape Project</a>, the research group Schafer started at Simon Fraser University, Vancouver, in the late 1960s. See Barry Truax (ed.), <em>Handbook for Acoustic Ecology</em> (1978).</p>
    <p><sup>3</sup>R. Murray Schafer, <em>The Tuning of the World</em> (1977), reissued as <em>The Soundscape: Our Sonic Environment and the Tuning of the World</em> (1994).</p>
    <p><sup>4</sup>P.S. I tried to play 4 games at the same time and also tried to control/manipulate the sounds and visuals.</p>
    <p><sup>5</sup>P.S. You can try virtual field recording by yourself by entering an open world game, then go to setting and mute the music sound leaving only ambient and game sound.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-6 pt-4" style="border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 2rem;">
    <p class="text-sm" set:html={credits1}></p>
    <p class="text-sm" set:html={credits1b}></p>
    <p class="text-sm" set:html={credits2}></p>
    <p class="text-sm" set:html={credits3}></p>
    <p class="text-sm" set:html={credits4}></p>
  </div>
  <p class="text-sm mt-6" set:html={credits5}></p>

  <div class="art-media-grid npcs-grid">
    <div class="art-media-full npcs-row npcs-row-design">
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/do-npcs-hear/model.png"
          alt="Do The NPCs Hear The Simulated Wind — aluminum profile structure model render"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="text-sm mt-2">
          Structure design model — aluminum profiles, 4-station setup
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/do-npcs-hear/npcs-promo.jpg"
          alt="Do The NPCs Hear The Simulated Wind — poster"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </figure>
    </div>

    <img src={photo("08")} alt={photoAlt("08")} class="art-media-full" loading="lazy" />

    {["06", "07", "04", "05", "11", "12", "02", "03"].map((n) => (
      <img src={photo(n)} alt={photoAlt(n)} loading="lazy" />
    ))}

    <div class="art-media-full npcs-row npcs-row-3">
      {["01", "09", "10"].map((n) => (
        <img src={photo(n)} alt={photoAlt(n)} loading="lazy" />
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  /* equal columns so the 16:9 photos line up row by row */
  .npcs-grid {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr !important;
  }
  .npcs-row {
    display: grid;
    gap: 14px;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .npcs-row-3 {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  }
  /* square render beside 4:5 poster: 5fr/4fr gives both the same height */
  .npcs-row-design {
    grid-template-columns: 5fr 4fr;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .npcs-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr !important;
    }
  }
</style>
